# Track 4 — E2E round 4: 60 vCPU total budget
#
# Rebalanced (2026-06) after two docker stats rounds:
#   1. token-issuer (RS256 signing) was pinned, validators idle -> moved
#      budget validators -> issuer (2.4 -> 5.7 vCPU). Throughput 580 -> ~900/s.
#   2. proof-queue Redis then pinned at its 0.3 cap (it absorbs every
#      status-poll read) -> give it a full core; snark/token Redis stay small.
#
# | Service              | cpus x replicas | subtotal |
# |----------------------|-----------------|----------|
# | proof-queue Redis    | 1.0 x 1         | 1.0      |
# | snark+token Redis    | 0.2 x 2         | 0.4      |
# | request-handler      | 0.6 x 8         | 4.8      |
# | verifier-selector    | 1.0 x 1         | 1.0      |
# | token-issuer         | 0.95 x 6        | 5.7      |
# | snark-verifier       | 1.0 x 45        | 45.0     |
# | token-validator (x5) | 0.4 x 5         | 2.0      |
# | TOTAL                |                 | 59.9     |
#
# Manager (c5.4xlarge, 16 vCPU) hosts proof+snark+token Redis + handler +
# selector + issuer = ~12.9 vCPU. Validators run on the App EC2s.
#
# Load with:  set -a; source e2e-throughput-round4.env; set +a
PROOF_CPUS=1.0
REDIS_CPUS=0.2
HANDLER_REPLICAS=8
HANDLER_CPUS=0.6
SELECTOR_CPUS=1.0
ISSUER_REPLICAS=6
ISSUER_CPUS=0.95
SNARK_REPLICAS=45
SNARK_COUNT=45
# Applied manually on each App EC2: docker update --cpus=$VALIDATOR_CPUS ...
VALIDATOR_CPUS=0.4
